---
export const prerender = true;
import BaseThemeLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseThemeLayout.astro';
import ModeThemeToggle from '../../../components/ui/ModeThemeToggle.astro';
import AstroMarkdown from '../../../components/markdown/AstroMarkdown.astro';
import { parseMarkdown } from '@lossless-group/lfm';

// One markdown document, every callout type. Parsed and rendered exactly the
// way production pages render them — through `parseMarkdown()` + AstroMarkdown.
// If anything looks wrong here, it's also wrong in production.
const sampleMd = `## All ten callout types

> [!note] Note
> Default-weight neutral callout. Used for asides, soft references, and "good to know" content that doesn't need an accent color.

> [!info] Why this design system page exists
> This is the brand's *hero* callout — bigger, brighter, glowy edges in vibrant mode and dialed-back in light mode. Use it sparingly: one per page, at the top, for the "why this matters" beat.
>
> - It supports lists.
> - It supports multiple paragraphs.
> - It supports inline \`code samples\` with accent-tinted backgrounds.
>
> Mode-awareness rides on the existing \`--fx-glow-opacity\` and \`--fx-glow-spread\` tokens, so the glow scales automatically per mode.

> [!tip] Tip
> Lime accent. Good for "if you're stuck, try this" hints — adjacent to but distinct from \`success\`.

> [!success] Success
> Lime accent (same family as tip). Use for confirmations and "this worked" outcomes.

> [!warning] Warning
> Amber accent. Use for non-fatal hazards: \`> [!warn]\` and \`> [!caution]\` are aliases that route here.

> [!danger] Danger
> Magenta accent. Use for destructive actions, breaking changes, and errors. Aliases: \`fail\`, \`failure\`, \`error\`, \`bug\`.

> [!quote] On showing your work
> Show your work-in-progress. The act of showing is the work.
>
> — adapted from Austin Kleon

> [!example] Example
> Violet accent. Use to set off a worked example from surrounding prose.

> [!question] FAQ
> Cyan accent. Aliases: \`faq\`, \`help\`. Pair with \`answer\` body text below the header.

> [!important] Important
> Magenta accent. Visually similar to danger but semantically distinct — use when the reader *must* read this, not when something is broken.

## Header-less variants

Some authors want the colored container without a label. Use \`[!info]-\` (Obsidian's foldable-empty syntax) or supply an explicit empty title:

> [!tip]
> Without a custom title, the type's default label appears. ("Tip" here.)

## Lists, paragraphs, and code inside callouts

Tailwind v4 preflight zeros out \`<p>\` margins and list bullets globally. The callout stylesheet restores prose flow scoped to the callout body so multi-paragraph + bulleted content renders correctly:

> [!info] Multi-paragraph + lists
> First paragraph establishes the point.
>
> Second paragraph adds the detail. \`Inline code\` reads at 0.9em with a tinted background.
>
> 1. Numbered lists work
> 2. Bullets work
>    - Including nested ones
> 3. And consecutive list items have proper spacing

## Aliases

Authors don't have to remember canonical names. The callout-types registry maps:

- \`warn\`, \`caution\`, \`attention\` → warning
- \`fail\`, \`failure\`, \`error\`, \`bug\` → danger
- \`hint\` → tip
- \`check\`, \`done\` → success
- \`todo\` → note
- \`abstract\`, \`summary\`, \`tldr\` → info
- \`faq\`, \`help\` → question

Anything unrecognized falls back to \`note\`.
`;

const tree = await parseMarkdown(sampleMd);
---

<BaseThemeLayout
  title="Design System · Callouts"
  description="All ten callout types rendered by the LFM Callout component, with mode-awareness, aliases, and the info hero variant."
  noindex={true}
  containerClass="px-6 py-12 max-w-4xl mx-auto"
>
  <header class="mb-8">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3" style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-family: var(--font-code);">
      Design System · Markdown · Callouts
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-3" style="font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--color-text);">
      Callouts
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg max-w-3xl mb-2" style="color: var(--color-text-muted); line-height: 1.55;">
      Every type below is rendered through the production LFM pipeline — <code style="font-family: var(--font-code); color: var(--color-primary);">parseMarkdown()</code> → <code style="font-family: var(--font-code); color: var(--color-primary);">AstroMarkdown</code> → <code style="font-family: var(--font-code); color: var(--color-primary);">Callout.astro</code>. Toggle the mode to see light / dark / vibrant adaptations.
    </p>
    <p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-family: var(--font-code); font-size: 0.8125rem;">
      Canonical: <code style="color: var(--color-primary);">packages/lfm-astro/components/Callout.astro</code> · See also <a href="/design-system/markdown" style="color: var(--color-primary); text-decoration: underline;">Markdown components index</a>.
    </p>
  </header>

  <ModeThemeToggle />

  <article class="callout-stage prose-stage">
    <AstroMarkdown node={tree} />
  </article>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3" style="font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--color-text);">Authoring syntax</h2>
    <pre class="syntax-pre">{`> [!info] Optional title
> Body content. Supports **markdown**, lists, code, multiple paragraphs.
>
> - bullet
> - bullet
>
> Trailing paragraph works too.`}</pre>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3" style="font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--color-text);">Adding a new type</h2>
    <ol class="ds-numbered">
      <li>Add an entry to <code>CALLOUT_TYPES</code> in <code>callout-types.ts</code> (label + iconPaths).</li>
      <li>Add a <code>.ak-callout--&#123;type&#125;</code> rule in <code>callout.css</code> setting <code>--callout-accent</code> to a Tier-1 named color from <code>theme.css</code>.</li>
      <li>Add light-mode and (if a loud color) dark-mode overrides in the same file.</li>
      <li>Optionally add aliases in the <code>ALIASES</code> map.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</BaseThemeLayout>

<style is:global>
  .callout-stage {
    padding: 2rem;
    border-radius: 1rem;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .callout-stage h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .callout-stage h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .callout-stage p {
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0.75rem 0;
  }
  .callout-stage ul,
  .callout-stage ol {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0.75rem 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .callout-stage ul { list-style: disc; }
  .callout-stage ol { list-style: decimal; }
  .callout-stage li { margin: 0.25rem 0; }
  .callout-stage code {
    font-family: var(--font-code);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 10%, transparent);
    color: var(--color-primary);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
  }
</style>

<style>
  .syntax-pre {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-family: var(--font-code);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .ds-numbered {
    color: var(--color-text);
    list-style: decimal;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .ds-numbered li {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }
  .ds-numbered code {
    font-family: var(--font-code);
    color: var(--color-primary);
    font-size: 0.875em;
  }
</style>
